#VRML_SIM R2025a utf8

# The first HEIGHTFIELD TERRAIN world in OmniSim. An ElevationGrid boundingObject is
# registered with newton as a native heightfield collider (newton.Heightfield +
# ModelBuilder.add_shape_heightfield) instead of falling off the end of OmSolid's
# geometry dispatch chain unrecognised.
#
# WHY THIS WORLD EXISTS. Until 2026-08-14 an ElevationGrid registered NO Newton collider
# at all -- it derives from OmGeometry, not OmTriangleMeshGeometry, so the boundingObject
# if/else chain simply ran off the end. Silently: no warning, exit 0. Measured on
# tests/benchmarks/omnibench/lane4/worlds/object_elevationgrid_terrain.wbt, a 1 kg sphere
# dropped on a flat grid reached z = -42.89 m and was STILL ACCELERATING at t = 3 s.
# (Before 2026-08-12 the implicit z=0 ground plane masked the same defect as a
# plausible-looking rest at z = 0.0996, which is why it survived so long.) That probe now
# scores WORKS.
#
# WHAT TO EXPECT. The terrain is a BOWL: basin at z = -0.94, rising to z = 0.70 at the
# middle of each edge and z = 2.33 at the corners, over 25x25 samples spanning 14.4 x 14.4 m.
# Four balls drop from z = 3.0 onto different points of the slope, land, ROLL DOWNHILL and
# collect in the basin.
#
# ⚠ THE BOWL SHAPE IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT DECORATION. The first draft of this world used
# rolling hills and asserted a per-ball rest height under each drop point. That was wrong
# twice over, and measuring it is what caught it: balls do not stay where they land on a
# sloped surface (three of the four rolled into a common hollow), and the fourth rolled
# clean off the 12 m edge and fell to z = -981. A heightfield is a FINITE PATCH with no
# skirt and no implicit floor beneath it -- a body that leaves the footprint falls for
# ever. The raised edge is what makes the claim below true.
#
# THE CLAIM, numeric rather than visual:
#   * every ball rests ABOVE the basin floor (-0.94) and below the edge (0.70)
#   * every ball stays INSIDE the footprint (|x| and |y| < 7.2)
#   * every ball ends LOWER than it started -- it rolled downhill, so the surface really
#     is sloped and is not a flat plane that happens to sit at the right height
# A ball resting at one common height regardless of where it landed would mean the
# elevation data is being ignored; a ball below the basin floor means the collider is
# missing again. Run
#
#   python -m omnisim run-headless <this world> --duration 20 --fail-on-runaway
#
# which fails loudly on the second case.

EXTERNPROTO "omnisim://projects/objects/backgrounds/protos/OmniSimSky.proto"
EXTERNPROTO "omnisim://projects/objects/lights/protos/OmniSimSun.proto"
EXTERNPROTO "omnisim://projects/objects/lights/protos/OmniSimSunMarker.proto"

WorldInfo {
  gravity 9.81
  basicTimeStep 8
  coordinateSystem "ENU"
  newtonSolver "mujoco"
  newtonStatics TRUE
  newtonGroundMu 1
}
Viewpoint {
  position -13 -13 9
  orientation 0.33 -0.26 -0.91 1.16
}
OmniSimSky {
}
DEF SUN OmniSimSun {
}
DEF SUN_MARKER OmniSimSunMarker {
}
# xSpacing/ySpacing are CELL sizes, so 25 samples at 0.6 m span 0.6*(25-1) = 14.4 m per
# axis, with the grid's (0,0) CORNER -- not its centre -- at `translation`.
